data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Recommendation: keep `mode` as an explicit dependency.** `alerts.ts` shouldn't import runtime config. Read `emergencyMode` once at the worker's entry point and pass it in.\n\n**Why**\n• **Easy to test:** `alerts` stays a pure function. Tests just pass `true` or `false`, with no module mocking and no reset between tests.\n• **No stale values:** if `alerts` imports config, the value can get locked in when the module first loads. That breaks if config comes from env or bindings at request time, which is common in workers.\n• **The dependency is visible:** every caller can see that alert behavior depends on mode, and the compiler flags any caller that forgets it.\n• **Cleaner imports:** a low-level module like `alerts` doesn't depend on config loading, so you avoid import cycles.\n\n**Shape:** make the argument narrow and required, e.g. `sendAlert(event, { emergencyMode }: { emergencyMode: boolean })` or `Pick<Config, \"emergencyMode\">`. Don't give it a default, or a forgotten argument will silently mean \"not emergency.\" If threading it through many layers gets noisy, pass a small context object instead of switching to a direct import.\n\n**Test strategy**\n1. **`alerts` unit tests:** a table-driven test that runs each alert case with emergency on and off. No mocks.\n2. **`config` unit tests:** check how `emergencyMode` is parsed: the default when it's unset, true and false values, and invalid input.\n3. **One wiring test at the entry point:** set emergency mode in the env or bindings, run the handler, and check that you get emergency behavior. This is the only test that checks config and alerts together.\n4. **Type check in CI:** run `tsc --noEmit` so a missing `mode` argument fails the build.\n5. **Don't mock the config module** (e.g. with `vi.mock`). Needing that is a sign the dependency has gone hidden again."}}

